WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:04.269
So in conjunction with our post-processing volume

2
00:00:04.269 --> 00:00:06.750
parameters, we also have the ability

3
00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:10.910
to add custom post-processing volume materials.

4
00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:14.310
These materials can be added to the post-processing volume

5
00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.350
via the post-processing materials input.

6
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.390
Now, we're not going to step through this class

7
00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:22.510
on how to go create a custom robust post-processing

8
00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:23.510
material.

9
00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:26.030
However, there is one included.

10
00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:28.839
So if I go down to our content drawer,

11
00:00:28.839 --> 00:00:31.359
and within our materials custom folder,

12
00:00:31.399 --> 00:00:34.200
there is this MPPV basic.

13
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.399
And if I open this up, I want to just show real quick

14
00:00:37.399 --> 00:00:39.840
a couple of different things.

15
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.159
So the material creation process

16
00:00:41.159 --> 00:00:42.679
itself is very straightforward.

17
00:00:42.679 --> 00:00:46.079
Again, very much material 101, the difference

18
00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:48.840
being that we will leverage some different nodes that

19
00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.920
allow us to inject information within our post-process.

20
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.000
But things like these vector parameters

21
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.600
where we can expose them to be able to control them,

22
00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.979
that, again, is all very much material 101.

23
00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:07.379
As we saw with our localized fog volume,

24
00:01:07.379 --> 00:01:09.700
in particular the material that we created for that,

25
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.260
we have some similar things that we

26
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.659
need to change as well for our post-processing material.

27
00:01:14.659 --> 00:01:17.019
So if I select the main material output

28
00:01:17.019 --> 00:01:19.900
itself or anywhere in the blank space of the graph,

29
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:21.379
the important thing to note here

30
00:01:21.379 --> 00:01:24.739
is that we have to change our material domain to post-process.

31
00:01:24.739 --> 00:01:26.260
And by doing this, you'll notice

32
00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:28.180
that it removes a lot of our properties

33
00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:31.859
and only allows for emissive color.

34
00:01:31.859 --> 00:01:33.620
By changing the material domain, we're

35
00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.659
now going to propagate information

36
00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:38.980
into the post-processing domain

37
00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.260
as it's going through all the different steps

38
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:42.739
to render the image.

39
00:01:42.739 --> 00:01:44.140
So if you don't have that checked,

40
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:45.579
it's obviously not going to work,

41
00:01:45.579 --> 00:01:48.620
so make sure your material is set to post-process.

42
00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:52.019
And again, this is just a very basic example

43
00:01:52.019 --> 00:01:53.980
I've created as a parent material.

44
00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:55.739
If I go back down to our content drawer,

45
00:01:55.739 --> 00:01:58.420
I've simply right-clicked Create Material Instance,

46
00:01:58.459 --> 00:02:01.219
and now we have this MIPPV basic.

47
00:02:01.219 --> 00:02:02.659
So this is the material that we're

48
00:02:02.659 --> 00:02:06.700
going to apply in our post-processing volume.

49
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.099
All right, so with my post-processing volume

50
00:02:09.099 --> 00:02:11.419
selected, and I do have my content drawer,

51
00:02:11.419 --> 00:02:13.919
I have this selected as well.

52
00:02:13.919 --> 00:02:16.740
So if I scroll down, I'm looking

53
00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.979
for in the rendering features post-processing materials.

54
00:02:19.979 --> 00:02:23.099
To add this material, simply click the little plus button,

55
00:02:23.099 --> 00:02:24.419
which will add an element.

56
00:02:24.419 --> 00:02:25.259
And in this case, we're going

57
00:02:25.259 --> 00:02:27.099
to do an asset reference because we're

58
00:02:27.139 --> 00:02:31.419
going to reference one of the assets in our content browser.

59
00:02:31.419 --> 00:02:33.860
And I could do this little use selected asset

60
00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.099
from content browser, and because it's selected,

61
00:02:36.099 --> 00:02:40.090
it will apply, or you can select from the dropdown.

62
00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:42.009
One pro tip that I do recommend

63
00:02:42.009 --> 00:02:46.090
is as you're creating post-processing volume materials,

64
00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:49.889
add something like a underscore PPV in your material,

65
00:02:49.889 --> 00:02:51.849
because you notice the engine will show you

66
00:02:51.849 --> 00:02:54.530
every material that is available,

67
00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:56.250
which really all we want is those

68
00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:01.090
that will be applied within the post-processing volume domain.

69
00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:03.490
All right, let me scroll back down here.

70
00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:04.770
So you can see that we do get this slider,

71
00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:07.009
which essentially is just an opacity slider.

72
00:03:07.009 --> 00:03:10.689
So this allows me to essentially fade

73
00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:15.050
the actual material itself on top of our scene.

74
00:03:16.569 --> 00:03:17.689
Let's just take a look real fast

75
00:03:17.689 --> 00:03:19.969
at the material instance that I have here.

76
00:03:19.969 --> 00:03:23.099
So I created a couple of vector parameters.

77
00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:25.300
I'll just move this off here, color blend,

78
00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.900
and then we also have this overlay base.

79
00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.460
So if I just kind of reset some of these to white,

80
00:03:31.389 --> 00:03:32.229
there we go.

81
00:03:32.229 --> 00:03:33.550
So color overlay control,

82
00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:36.150
which again is just like a granular opacity,

83
00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.830
so we don't have to do within the material

84
00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:41.289
or the post-processing material slot.

85
00:03:41.289 --> 00:03:42.770
Color blend will be something that like goes

86
00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:45.250
over the top of this, and of course our base.

87
00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:48.210
So I could do something like green.

88
00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:52.979
We'll use some very, very big colors here and hot pink.

89
00:03:52.979 --> 00:03:54.979
And then we'll do something like this,

90
00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:56.860
0.5 to blend between the two.

91
00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.180
And you'll notice now kind of adjust.

92
00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:02.139
Again, this is not a material that I would say

93
00:04:02.139 --> 00:04:04.620
is something that would go into final production.

94
00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.460
But the key here is that again,

95
00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:08.780
by creating these materials

96
00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:10.819
and injecting them in the post-processing,

97
00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:13.060
we've got just additional control

98
00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:15.139
outside of just the properties and parameters

99
00:04:15.139 --> 00:04:19.850
that the post-processing volume affords us.

100
00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:20.689
In the next video,

101
00:04:20.689 --> 00:04:22.850
we'll walk through lighting an interior scene

102
00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:24.250
from start to finish.
